  videoPauseButton: {fileID: 1424935237}
  artworks:
  - imageName: 2868 Angki Purbandono  _TV Lovers Indonesia 2004-2010_  Digital Print
      on Acrylic Sheet  234 Neon box Installastion  350x700cm
    aliases:
    - qr-tv
    title: TV Lovers
    description: TODO add description
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: e8e4c5e7659eb77b2c764cfd24b4bdc9, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 2b5cce10a7a29284c914f06478969c27, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: 0626 Entang W, Me as Teddy Bear. M
    aliases: []
    title: Me as a Teddy Bear
    description: "Entang was a classmate of Nasirun. He made this work after 9/11,
      when the Twin Towers were hit and burned. At the time, Entang\u2014 who has
      an American wife \u2014 was abroad for an exhibition, not in the US. When he
      returned, the atmosphere in America was very tense, and he was searched on
      arrival, an unpleasant experience. Afterward, he made this painting. The teddy
      bear is a symbol of America, and he depicted himself as one \u2014 an Indonesian
      in America who, despite this, was still treated with suspicion as a foreigner.
      It was a difficult time for him and for many others, especially those who looked
      like they might be from the Middle East, who could easily be regarded with
      suspicion, as if associated with terrorism.\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 8e10201086e69512e23d186dd667e724, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 1f9f13d25e1c8834fa69ec678ddf609c, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: 2631 Handiwirman, Akhir Pekan dan Projek Organik dari Tak Berakar
      Tak Berpucuk #1 Acrylic on canvas 300 x 280 cm 2012 M
    aliases: []
    title: Akhir Pekan
    description: TODO add description
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 5f4f0aee1d6a314d980a9fb6996eac9c, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5f85b09427905d646ba0e8f9045c3616, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: Gb.55. 2428 Hendra Gunawan, Aloen-Aloen KIdul _Cat minyak diatas canvas_225x290
      cm
    aliases: []
    title: Aloen-Aloen Kidul
    description: "Hendra's paintings often featured the lively Pasar Malam (night
      market), a recurring subject in his work. This particular painting depicts
      the night market at Alun-Alun Kidul, near the Keraton in Yogyakarta, and was
      completed in 1946. The artwork has since been restored and cleaned, revealing
      much brighter colors than when it was first acquired.\r\nComparing this painting
      with one Hendra created 35 years later titled \u201CNight Market\u201D shows
      a remarkable evolution in his style. The later work is much larger, brighter,
      and more visually dramatic, reflecting the wider availability of vivid pigments.
      While the 1946 painting remains relatively realistic, it already hints at Hendra's
      growing experimentation through subtle deformation of figures and expressive,
      non-naturalistic colors, such as painting a man's face blue. These bold artistic
      choices became increasingly pronounced in his later works, making the comparison
      especially revealing of his artistic development.\r\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n"
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 95e8cba5ea7138b0ebbd5685ed79c286, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 7fe105b76084e0e4d9986a9803ed38ca, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: 2041 Hendra Gunawan, Night Market M
    aliases: []
    title: Night Market
    description: "Night Market is one of Hendra's monumental paintings, measuring
      approximately 4 \xD7 2 meters. Created around 35 years after his earlier Pasar
      Malam scene titled \u201CAloe Alooen Kidul\u201D , it demonstrates the remarkable
      evolution of his artistic style. Painted after his release from prison, the
      work is filled with bold, vibrant colors and expressive, stylized figures that
      showcase his technical mastery and confident use of distortion.\r\nThe painting
      captures the lively atmosphere of a Pasar Malam (night market), where children
      play, vendors sell food and goods, and families gather to enjoy the festivities.
      Through its dynamic composition and joyful energy, Hendra celebrates the warmth
      of communal life and the enduring cultural significance of the night market,
      while reflecting the more expressive and daring visual language that defined
      his later works.\r\n\n\n\n\n"
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: f3b59fa662c78abd6f00c069617805d8, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 0c0761d5624e50947971d0d488137dbb, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: 0704 Hendra Gunawan, Self Portrait M
    aliases: []
    title: Self Portrait
    description: 'Self Portrait is one of the many self-portraits Hendra painted
      during his imprisonment. These works offer a deeply personal glimpse into his
      emotional and psychological state, often depicting him in quiet contemplation.
      Surrounding his figure are strange, ghost-like forms, which may symbolize the
      inner fears, anxieties, or disturbing thoughts he experienced during his isolation.

      This
      particular painting is especially significant because it once belonged to Adam
      Malik, Indonesia''s former Vice President and a renowned art collector. Interestingly,
      the work is unsigned. Many of Hendra''s paintings from this period either lack
      a signature or had their original signatures removed, as collectors feared
      persecution due to Hendra''s alleged association with the Indonesian Communist
      Party after the 1965 political upheaval. Although some signatures were later
      added, they are not always authentic. As the speaker notes, the true value
      of the work lies in the painting itself rather than the signature, since a
      signature can be forged, but Hendra''s artistic expression cannot.




'
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 839cc9da2db3abcdb7cf8ced5661e515, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 521647f08ed66004ea91cda0066d2751, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: sketches by hendra
    aliases: []
    title: Sketches of Hendra Gunawan
    description: "These sketches, created by Hendra in the early 1940s\u2014particularly
      between 1941 and 1946\u2014are exceptionally rare. During this period, art
      materials were scarce, so Hendra was often unable to turn these sketches into
      finished paintings. As a result, they remain unique, one-of-a-kind works.\r\nThe
      sketches were drawn on old, previously used paper, with typed stories on the
      reverse side. While they may not have the same financial value as Hendra's
      paintings, their historical significance, rarity, and artistic value make them
      invaluable. The large number of these sketches has also impressed many experts
      who have visited the collection.\r\n\n\n\n\n"
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 70aaea3fc010d4c59051c0052f054421, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5c40171fa5a7d253453fd8b9f23c68a4, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: Hendra Gunawan_Two Guerilla Fighters_c1949_Oil on board_159 x 118
      cm
    aliases: []
    title: Two Guerilla Fighters
    description: "This is another strong work by Hendra from the 1940s revolutionary
      period. It has no signature \u2014 a large signature was on it when acquired,
      but it wasn't Hendra's and didn't match the painting's date, so it was erased
      by a conservator.\r\nThis is one of the strongest works in OHD's collection
      from the revolution period, depicting guerrilla fighters. That period interests
      OHD greatly, who believes he may hold the largest number of works from it.
      The expression is genuine because Hendra was fighting himself at the time,
      still in the war \u2014 unlike his later retrospective works on the same period,
      done after the fighting had ended.\r\nMaterials were often poor quality since
      canvas and paint were scarce; artists used whatever they could find. This piece
      is painted on paper, which was then mounted onto a wood panel.\r\n"
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: de0922189b58357a7e67adb18cbb4e35, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 622986008c85350499b771867eee66ea, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: 0633 I Made Djirna, White Eyes
    aliases: []
    title: White Eyes
    description: TODO add description
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 10da1fd8fe9ef6bfa1722bd78625f5f2, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: f51e696275bb7ca40a212ca03c550787, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: 0644 Nasirun, Melarung
    aliases: []
    title: Melarung
    description: "Nasirun is one of OHD's favorite artists among the younger generation,
      though not so young anymore. Wayang, the Javanese puppet tradition, is a recurring
      subject in his work, reflecting his expertise in the art form. This large painting
      \u2014 3 by 5 meters, a single canvas \u2014 depicts a time when things weren't
      going well for him, and he felt there was something he needed to throw away,
      perhaps the cause of the misery he was experiencing. \"Melarung\" means throwing
      something into the sea, ridding oneself of unwanted things. Beyond its subject,
      the painting's scale alone makes it striking and eye-catching, and the red
      tones are highly typical of Nasirun's work"
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 3706b5dd100d79b89e3608d47d9c89ea, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: c649bfaa71794c74ba770b9c0c332859, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: PL.22. Soedibio_Beneath the Death  Angel_1947_Oil on canvas_195x140
      cm
    aliases: []
    title: Death Beneath the Angel
    description: "Soedibio belonged to the same generation of artists as Sujojono,
      Gunawan, as well as Affandi. Affandi, however, was the oldest among them and
      always stood apart, following his own distinct path in his work. The others
      \u2014 Sudjojono, Sujoyono, Gunawan, and Hariyani \u2014 influenced one another
      over the years, and as a result, some similarities can be traced across their
      paintings.\r\nSoedibio, though, was different in one key respect: his subject
      matter and style, rooted in surrealism, set him apart from all the others.
      In particular, the surrealist violence he depicted from the Second World War
      is extraordinary \u2014 the intensity and imagination behind these works go
      far beyond what one would expect.\r\nThis is something that has been noticed
      not only by OHD but by nearly every curator and art director who has visited
      the collection, including those from abroad. Sudjojono's work is consistently
      singled out as one of the highlights of the collection, because this particular
      kind of surrealist expression could only have come from him. He is regarded
      as the father and pioneer of surrealism within Indonesian modern art, a distinction
      that sets his contribution apart from that of his contemporaries.\r\n"
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: f3b8b293c994b13f3d356e6257c0f649, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 539d432b26b4a7e4082284f94f436ef2, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
  - imageName: 0751 S. Sudjojono, A Lakeside Meeting in the Mountains
    aliases: []
    title: Lakeside Meeting in the Mountains
    description: "This is a very good painting, previously owned by Affandi \u2014
      OHD acquired it from Affandi's collection. It's another typical satirical work
      by Sudjojono, mocking the \"new rich,\" whose conduct doesn't match the wealth
      they possess. The only figure meant to represent a true, ideal woman of high
      status is the one at the center, his wife Rosemary. The others, though possibly
      just as rich or richer, don't carry themselves in a way that reflects that
      status. Sudjojono always brought this humorous perspective, and he expressed
      it exceptionally well in his paintings."
    audio: {fileID: 8300000, guid: 36155cf3baa2a1bd7128f24bd0813c06, type: 3}
    image: {fileID: 21300000, guid: b1b7fdf9a30e6244392175b0e40faf31, type: 3}
    videoPrefab: {fileID: 0}
